\details{
One dendrogram is computed with \code{stats::hclust()} and cut at each
candidate \code{k} with \code{stats::cutree()}. A partition is eligible only
if all its clusters contain at least \code{min_size} surveys. Ineligible
partitions remain in the output, with \code{NA} criterion values, and do
not contribute to the silhouette recommendation or elbow curve. If no
candidate partition is eligible, a warning is issued and the diagnostic
output is returned with \code{NA} for the suggested \code{k} and its value.
The distance, dendrogram, group assignments, and cluster-size diagnostics
remain available. The size constraint is not relaxed automatically.
All-zero pairwise dissimilarities still cause an error.

The criteria describe partitions of this particular dendrogram, within
the candidate range and size constraint. Neither guarantees a unique
or ecologically meaningful classification.
Inspect the curves, group sizes, species composition, and ecological
interpretation before choosing a final cut. A silhouette optimum at an
endpoint may justify exploring a wider candidate range.

\strong{Mean silhouette width: maximize.}
For survey \eqn{i}, let \eqn{a(i)} be its mean dissimilarity to the other
members of its cluster and \eqn{b(i)} its smallest mean dissimilarity to
another cluster. Its silhouette width is
\deqn{s(i) = \frac{b(i) - a(i)}{\max\{a(i), b(i)\}}.}
The mean over all surveys is calculated with \code{cluster::silhouette()}.
The eligible \code{k} with the largest mean is recommended. Individual
widths lie in \eqn{[-1, 1]}: values near 1 indicate good separation,
values near zero indicate overlap, and negative values suggest that a
survey is closer, on average, to another group. See Rousseeuw (1987).
When \code{min_size = 1}, singleton surveys receive a silhouette width of
zero, following the convention in \code{cluster::silhouette()}. All surveys,
including these singletons, contribute to the overall mean; a high mean
silhouette does not by itself establish the ecological value of a singleton.

\strong{Elbow: look for a change in slope, not the minimum dispersion.}
For each eligible partition, \code{fpc::cluster.stats()} calculates
\code{within.cluster.ss} directly from the chosen dissimilarities
\eqn{d_{ij}} as
\deqn{W(k) = \sum_{r=1}^{k} \frac{1}{2 n_r}
      \sum_{i \in C_r}\sum_{j \in C_r} d_{ij}^{2},}
where \eqn{C_r} is cluster \eqn{r} and \eqn{n_r} is its size. For Euclidean
distances, this is exactly the within-cluster sum of squares. This includes
chord distance, for which the equivalence holds on the normalized data.
For other dissimilarities it is a distance-based dispersion summary;
a variance or centroid interpretation is not assumed. For non-Euclidean
dissimilarities, a monotone decrease is not guaranteed.

The elbow idea seeks diminishing gains in compactness as \code{k} increases
(Thorndike, 1953). Inspect the plot for a transition from a steep decrease
to a flatter curve. There may be no clear elbow, and its appearance depends
on the candidate range and grid. No automatic elbow location is returned:
\code{suggested_k} contains only the silhouette recommendation. At least
three eligible candidate values are needed to assess a change in slope.
A warning is issued if fewer are available, if dispersion is constant, or
if it increases beyond numerical tolerance. Squared dissimilarities must
remain finite. For exact ties in mean silhouette width, the smallest
\code{k} is selected.

Distance-based calculations use \code{vegan}, \code{cluster}, and \code{fpc};
the dendrogram and cuts use \code{stats}. Davies--Bouldin,
Calinski--Harabasz, and gap statistics are not returned.
A full distance matrix is held in memory, so this function is intended for
data sets that fit comfortably in memory.

\strong{Distances and agglomeration.}
No ordination or correction of dissimilarities is performed. The user must
choose a suitable distance and linkage combination. In particular,
\code{"ward.D2"} requires Euclidean distances for the usual Ward
minimum-variance interpretation; \code{"ward.D"} is a different algorithm.
Centroid and median linkage require care regarding Euclidean versus squared
Euclidean inputs and may produce dendrogram inversions; see
\code{stats::hclust()}. With the default quantitative options,
\code{distance = "jaccard"} does not convert abundances to presence/absence.
For binary analysis, supply a presence/absence database explicitly.
